From 8ed01f147e52045e98346539f2b7cb976c952a27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Srinivasan Koona Lokabiraman <srinivasan@nexthop.ai>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 08:33:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] bgpd: recover from SendHoldTimer expiry instead of wedging
 the peer
Upstream: yes
References: bsc#1290385,CVE-2026-120033,gh#FRRouting/frr#23945,gh#FRRouting/frr#24415

When the SendHoldTimer (RFC 9687) expires,
bgp_event_stop_with_notify() called bgp_stop() inline. bgp_stop()
runs event_cancel_event_ready(), which cancels the BGP_Stop event
that bgp_write_notify() had just queued to advance the FSM, and it
does not itself change state. The peer is therefore left stuck in
Established with a closed fd (fd == -1) and no ConnectRetry timer --
it never resets or reconnects.

Send the notification and let the FSM dispatch the queued BGP_Stop
(or TCP_fatal_error) so the peer tears down and reconnects like
every other stop.

Signed-off-by: Srinivasan Koona Lokabiraman <srinivasan@nexthop.ai>

diff --git a/bgpd/bgp_fsm.c b/bgpd/bgp_fsm.c
index 5581fae1c6..82256e8d6d 100644
--- a/bgpd/bgp_fsm.c
+++ b/bgpd/bgp_fsm.c
@@ -3675,5 +3675,15 @@ void bgp_event_stop_with_notify(struct event *event)
 {
 	struct peer_connection *connection = EVENT_ARG(event);
 
-	bgp_stop_with_notify(connection, BGP_NOTIFY_SEND_HOLD_ERR, 0);
+	/*
+	 * Drive the teardown through the FSM rather than stopping the peer
+	 * inline.  bgp_notify_send() -> bgp_write_notify() queues a BGP_Stop
+	 * (or TCP_fatal_error, if the stuck send queue rejects the write); the
+	 * FSM then advances the state and re-arms the ConnectRetry timer like
+	 * every other stop.  Calling bgp_stop() directly here (as
+	 * bgp_stop_with_notify() does) runs event_cancel_event_ready(), which
+	 * cancels that queued event, so the state is never advanced and the peer
+	 * is left stuck in Established with fd == -1 -- a zombie session.
+	 */
+	bgp_notify_send(connection, BGP_NOTIFY_SEND_HOLD_ERR, 0);
 }
